  "text": {
    "markdown": "## Changing a flat tire: roadside checklist\n\n**Stay safe first.** If you are on a motorway or a narrow shoulder with fast traffic, do not change the wheel there. Get everyone out on the side away from traffic, stand behind the barrier and call roadside assistance.\n\n### 1. Make the scene safe\n\n- [ ] Pull over on **flat, firm ground**, well away from traffic.\n- [ ] Switch on the **hazard lights** and put on a hi-vis vest if you have one.\n- [ ] Apply the **parking brake**. Put the car in Park (or first gear for a manual).\n- [ ] Get passengers out and well clear of the road.\n- [ ] Place the **warning triangle** about 45 m (50 yards) behind the car.\n- [ ] **Chock** the wheel diagonally opposite the flat with a wedge, brick or rock.\n\n### 2. Change the wheel\n\n- [ ] Get out the spare, jack and wheel wrench. Check the spare has air.\n- [ ] Remove the hubcap if there is one. **Loosen each wheel nut half a turn** (anticlockwise) while the tire is still on the ground. Do not remove them yet.\n- [ ] Put the jack under the **jacking point** nearest the flat (see the owner's manual) and raise the car until the tire is about 5 cm off the ground.\n- [ ] **Never put any part of your body under the car** while it is on the jack.\n- [ ] Remove the nuts, keep them together, and pull the wheel straight off.\n- [ ] Fit the spare and **hand-tighten** the nuts in a **star pattern** (opposite nuts in turn, not round the circle).\n- [ ] Lower the car until the tire touches the ground, then tighten the nuts fully with the wrench, again in a star pattern.\n- [ ] Lower the car completely, remove the jack and give each nut a final check.\n\n### 3. Before you drive off\n\n- [ ] Stow the flat, jack, wrench, triangle and chock.\n- [ ] Check the spare's pressure at the next fuel station.\n- [ ] If it is a narrow **space-saver spare**, stay under **80 km/h (50 mph)** and replace it as soon as you can.\n- [ ] Have the wheel nuts re-torqued after about 50 km.\n\n### Call for help instead if\n\n- You are on a motorway or cannot get clear of traffic.\n- The ground is soft or sloping, or the jack will not sit securely.\n- A wheel nut will not budge, or the car has no spare (many newer cars carry only a sealant kit).\n",
